WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.290
At the start of term, five teachers choose

2
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.910
their classes in order of seniority.

3
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.510
It feels fair.

4
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.880
Then Karim, who teaches maths well and

5
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.020
little else, ends up with a science class.

6
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.700
Today: matching teachers and classes, for

7
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.140
the best total and for the worst-off.

8
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.170
This is the assignment problem: each

9
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.330
class gets exactly one teacher, and each

10
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.330
teacher takes exactly one class.

11
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.810
A table scores how well each teacher

12
00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.490
suits each class, from zero to nine.

13
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.570
The goal is the best total score.

14
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.680
First come, first served: Amina chooses

15
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.460
first and takes maths, her favourite.

16
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.170
Karim, good only at maths, gets

17
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.330
science, with a score of two.

18
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.240
The total is twenty-nine.

19
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.220
The best total is thirty-nine.

20
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.860
Karim takes maths, Leila science, Yusuf

21
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.600
English, Zainab art, and Amina history.

22
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.370
The Hungarian method, from nineteen

23
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.250
fifty-five, finds it step by step,

24
00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.270
and a solver finds it too.

25
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.300
In CP-SAT, each teacher and class

26
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.640
pair gets a yes-or-no variable.

27
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.610
Exactly one yes in each row, and

28
00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.590
exactly one in each column.

29
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.300
Then maximise the scores of

30
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.040
the pairs you choose.

31
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.750
But the total is only one way to judge.

32
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.890
You can also ask: how good

33
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.610
is the worst match?

34
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.100
A plan with a high total can still give

35
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.680
one teacher a class they can hardly teach.

36
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:46.300
Quick check!

37
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.200
I want no teacher stuck with a

38
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.860
class they can hardly teach.

39
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.960
What should the model make

40
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.520
as large as possible?

41
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:56.840
The lowest score.

42
00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.170
Add a variable for the worst

43
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.070
match, keep every chosen pair at

44
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.410
or above it, and maximise it.

45
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.650
Here the best-total plan already does

46
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.990
well: its worst match is six.

47
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.510
On other tables, raising the worst

48
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.530
costs a little of the total.

49
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.050
Scores are opinions written as numbers.

50
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.430
Who wrote them, and did the

51
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.290
teachers see them?

52
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.530
Show both plans, the best total and the

53
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.690
kindest worst match, and decide together.

54
00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.690
Explain the rule you used before

55
00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:35.930
anyone sees their class.

56
00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.210
In the app, open “Teachers to classes”.

57
00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.630
Press “Let them choose in turn”, then

58
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.190
Run to watch the Hungarian method.

59
00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.200
Then change Karim’s maths score from

60
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.820
nine to five, and run both again.

61
00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:53.540
Your turn.

62
00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.730
The best-total plan gives Zainab, a new

63
00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.430
teacher, the class nobody wanted.

64
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.080
Nobody asked her about her scores.

65
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.180
What is the fair next step?

66
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.140
Check the scores with the people

67
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.360
they describe, then solve again.

68
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.520
Remember: one yes-or-no variable per pair,

69
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.100
and exactly one per row and per column.

70
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.660
First come can lose a lot.

71
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.240
And judge two ways: the

72
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.200
total, and the worst match.

73
00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.870
Try it in class tomorrow:

74
00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.510
share out five class jobs.

75
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.080
Pupils score each job from one to five,

76
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.360
and first they choose in turn.

77
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.940
Then the class looks for the best

78
00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.920
total, and for the plan where

79
00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.540
nobody gets a job they hate.
